{%- comment -%}
  THE SEARCH INDEX, built by Jekyll and served as one static file on this origin.

  IT IS PAGE-LEVEL AND NOT HEADING-LEVEL, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE REFUSAL rather than a shortcut.
  Heading-level results need an anchor per heading, and an anchor computed HERE would be computed by
  Liquid's `slugify`, while the anchors on the page are computed by kramdown. The two disagree --
  tests/test_internal_links_resolve.py measured 19 disagreements over 766 headings, every one of them
  a heading containing `--`, which kramdown folds into an en dash and drops. A search result carrying
  a slug from the wrong slugifier still returns 200 and still renders: it just lands the reader at
  the top of the page instead of at the heading it promised, reported by nobody. That is the exact
  failure shape this repository exists to refuse, so the index does not compute anchors at all.

  WHAT IS INDEXED: the title, the URL, and the page's FULL text with markup stripped.

  IT WAS THE FIRST 900 CHARACTERS FOR ABOUT TEN MINUTES, and the first query run against it is why
  it is not. Searching "collision" returned the landing page and Sequence allocation, and did not
  return Coordination or Quickstart -- the two pages the word is actually about -- because on those
  pages it first appears past the truncation. A search that misses the main page for a term is worse
  than no search at all: it answers confidently and it answers wrong, which is the failure shape
  this whole repository is about.

  THE COST OF THE FULL TEXT IS PAID BY THE READER WHO SEARCHES, AND ONLY THEM. assets/site.js
  fetches this lazily on the first focus of the search box rather than on page load, so it is off
  the critical path for every reader who never uses it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  FILTERED IN THE LOOP RATHER THAN BY `where_exp`. Liquid 4.0.4, which is what the pinned
  github-pages gem resolves to, parses no `or` inside a where_exp string and fails the whole build
  with "Expected end_of_string but found id". An `if` takes `or` perfectly well.
{%- endcomment -%}
[
{%- assign written = 0 -%}
{%- for p in site.pages -%}
{%- if p.url == '/' or p.url contains '.html' -%}
{%- if written > 0 %},{% endif -%}
{%- assign written = written | plus: 1 %}
{"t":{{ p.title | default: p.name | jsonify }},"u":{{ p.url | jsonify }},"x":{{ p.content | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | jsonify }}}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor %}
]
